//? Title: The Bootstrap: Standard Errors and Four Confidence Intervals
//? Subtitle: Replacement sampling, finite-distribution calibration and the iris median
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The nonparametric bootstrap replaces an unknown distribution by the empirical
distribution of the observations, then repeatedly samples from it with
replacement. We show a small draw, check the mean against its exact conditional
bootstrap variance, and retain the original iris Sepal.Length median experiment
with 4,000 resamples. Native C++ computes the statistics, bias, standard error
and percentile, basic, normal and BCa intervals. The final experiment separates
Monte Carlo error from the difference between the bootstrap and textbook mean
standard errors. Bootstrap validity depends on the statistic and sampling model;
increasing the resample count cannot establish that validity.
)

\toc

> The empirical distribution and a possible draw

For observations x1,...,xn, the empirical distribution puts mass 1/n on each
row, including repeated values. An ordinary bootstrap sample consists of n
independent draws from those rows with replacement. Evaluating the same
statistic on each sample gives replicates theta-star1,...,theta-starB.
This conditional distribution is used to approximate how the estimator would
vary across new samples from the unknown population. Whether that substitution
works requires assumptions beyond the computation itself.

The diagram shows one possible index sequence for {1,2,4}: {0,2,2}.
It is an illustration, rather than a trace of the random engine used below.
Repeated selection of the third row is allowed, and the second row is absent.
The program computes both statistics through the core API.

```{cpp, file="figures/bootstrap_draw.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<double> sample{1,2,4};
  const std::vector<std::size_t> indices{0,2,2};
  std::vector<double> resample;for (auto i:indices) resample.push_back(sample[i]);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.0;style.height=1.3;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<sample.size();++i) {
    const double y=3.0-3.0*i;
    diagram.node(std::format("x{}",i),{0,y},std::format("row {}: {}\nmass 1/3",i,sample[i]),style);
    style.fill={220,252,231};
    diagram.node(std::format("r{}",i),{5,y},std::format("draw {}: {}\nindex {}",i+1,resample[i],indices[i]),style);
    style.fill={255,255,255};
  }
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<indices.size();++i)
    diagram.edge(std::format("x{}",indices[i]),std::format("r{}",i));
  style.width=3.4;style.height=2.1;style.fill={219,234,254};
  diagram.node("stat",{10,0},std::format("resample: 1, 4, 4\nmean = {}\nmedian = {}",
      dm::mean(resample),dm::median(resample)),style);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<sample.size();++i) diagram.edge(std::format("r{}",i),"stat");
  diagram.text({5,5},"One possible draw with replacement; the original median is 2.")
      .save_svg("build/bootstrap_draw.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bootstrap_draw.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A possible resample from three equally weighted rows. The chosen indices are explicit; the later experiments use Bootstrap's random draws.)
\alttext(The original values are one, two and four. Row zero is selected once, row two twice and row one never. The resample one, four, four has mean three and median four.)

> Checking the theory on three observations

Let s be the sample standard deviation with denominator n-1. Conditional on
the observed sample, a single bootstrap draw has variance
(n-1)s²/n. Averaging n independent such draws gives the exact identity

$$
\operatorname{Var}_*(\bar X^*)=\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\bar x)^2
=\frac{n-1}{n^2}s^2,\qquad
\operatorname{se}_*(\bar X^*)=\frac{s\sqrt{n-1}}{n}.
$$
\alttext(The exact conditional bootstrap standard error of the mean is s times the square root of n minus one, divided by n.)

The usual estimated standard error s/sqrt(n) for a population mean is a
different quantity. The bootstrap reference is smaller by sqrt((n-1)/n).
For three observations the distinction is visible. The finite-B estimate is
the sample standard deviation of the replicates and fluctuates around the
conditional reference. No random seed can make it an exact identity.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<double> sample{1,2,4};
  const auto result=dm::stats::Bootstrap({20000,0.95,7}).run(sample,[](const auto& values){return dm::mean(values);});
  const double n=sample.size(),s=dm::sd(sample);
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n"
             "| original mean | {:.8f} |\n| bootstrap mean bias | {:.8f} |\n"
             "| bootstrap SE, B=20000 | {:.8f} |\n| exact conditional bootstrap SE | {:.8f} |\n"
             "| textbook mean SE | {:.8f} |\n",
      result.estimate,result.bias,result.standard_error,s*std::sqrt(n-1)/n,s/std::sqrt(n));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| quantity | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| original mean | 2.33333333 |
| bootstrap mean bias | -0.00543333 |
| bootstrap SE, B=20000 | 0.71202859 |
| exact conditional bootstrap SE | 0.72008230 |
| textbook mean SE | 0.88191710 |
// result_end
\caption(The conditional bootstrap reference is exact for this observed sample; the resampling estimate has Monte Carlo error.)

> Bias, standard error and interval definitions

Write theta-hat for the statistic on the original observations and m-star for
the mean of the B replicates. The estimated bias is m-star minus theta-hat.
The bootstrap SE uses the B-1 sample-variance denominator; B=1 is accepted
with SE zero, although one replicate cannot estimate variability reliably.
For level 1-alpha, let Q(p) denote a replicate quantile and z(p) a standard
normal quantile. Four intervals are returned:

- Percentile: [Q(alpha/2), Q(1-alpha/2)].
- Basic: [2 theta-hat - Q(1-alpha/2), 2 theta-hat - Q(alpha/2)].
- Normal: theta-hat - estimated bias, plus or minus z(1-alpha/2) times SE.
- BCa: [Q(p-low), Q(p-high)], using the adjustment below.

Quantiles linearly interpolate the sorted replicates at index (B-1)p, the
R type-7 convention. Finite-sample interpolation and discrete statistics mean
these endpoints do not give exact coverage simply by construction. Basic
reflection does not remove bias automatically; the normal interval also
assumes that a symmetric normal approximation is useful.

For BCa, theta-(i) denotes the statistic with row i removed, and
theta-dot their mean. The core uses strict replicate < theta-hat counts,
so ties affect the bias correction:

$$
z_0=\Phi^{-1}\left(\frac{\#\{\theta_b^*<\hat\theta\}}{B}\right),\qquad
a=\frac{\sum_i(\theta_{\cdot}-\theta_{(i)})^3}
{6\left[\sum_i(\theta_{\cdot}-\theta_{(i)})^2\right]^{3/2}},\qquad
p(q)=\Phi\left(z_0+\frac{z_0+z(q)}{1-a[z_0+z(q)]}\right).
$$
\alttext(BCa uses the normal quantile of the fraction of replicates strictly below the estimate, jackknife acceleration from centred cubed and squared deviations, and an adjusted normal probability for each nominal endpoint.)

Use p(alpha/2) and p(1-alpha/2) for the two endpoints. A constant jackknife
sequence uses a=0. BCa falls back to the percentile interval if the strict
fraction is zero or one, fewer than two jackknife values are available, or
the adjustment is nonfinite, singular, crosses its pole or reverses the
endpoint probabilities. Its higher-order accuracy theory needs regularity
conditions; this tied median example is not a demonstration of those conditions.

`Bootstrap::run` owns the resamples and delegates interval construction to
`stats::bootstrap_summary`, which also accepts externally generated finite
replicates and optional jackknife values. Original, resampled and jackknife
statistics must all be finite; observations themselves are left to the
callback's missing-value policy. Scaled jackknife deviations and widened
centred sums reduce numeric overflow, and unrepresentable summary endpoints
raise an exception instead of silently returning infinities.

> Original experiment: the iris Sepal.Length median

The built-in iris dataset supplies 150 complete sepal lengths in centimetres,
pooled across three species. This preserves the original univariate experiment:
B=4000, level=0.95 and seed=20240915. Treating the pooled rows as i.i.d. is
an illustrative resampling model; the calculation neither models species
structure nor establishes a sampling design for population inference.
`dm::median` copies the values and selects the middle order statistics without
changing the input. For even n it uses an overflow-safe midpoint.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bootstrap_iris_median.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();std::vector<double> x;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<iris.nrows();++i) x.push_back(iris.double_at("Sepal.Length",i));
  const auto result=dm::stats::Bootstrap({4000,0.95,20240915}).run(x,dm::median);
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n"
             "| observations | {} |\n| mean, cm | {:.8f} |\n| median, cm | {:.8f} |\n"
             "| sample SD, cm | {:.8f} |\n| bootstrap median bias, cm | {:.8f} |\n"
             "| bootstrap median SE, cm | {:.8f} |\n",
      x.size(),dm::mean(x),result.estimate,dm::sd(x),result.bias,result.standard_error);
  std::print("\n| nominal 95% interval | lower, cm | upper, cm |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [name,interval]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::pair<double,double>>>{
      {"percentile",result.percentile_interval},{"basic",result.basic_interval},
      {"normal, bias-adjusted centre",result.normal_interval},{"BCa",result.bca_interval}})
    std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n",name,interval.first,interval.second);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(result.replicates,24,"median replicates",dm::RGB{37,99,235})
      .title("Iris sepal-length median: 4,000 bootstrap replicates")
      .x_label("resampled median, cm").y_label("replicate count")
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/bootstrap_iris_median.svg");
}
```
