| bootstrap median SE, cm | 0.10177727 |

| nominal 95% interval | lower, cm | upper, cm |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| percentile | 5.60000000 | 6.00000000 |
| basic | 5.60000000 | 6.00000000 |
| normal, bias-adjusted centre | 5.61168271 | 6.01064229 |
| BCa | 5.60000000 | 6.00000000 |
\image(figures/bootstrap_iris_median.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The histogram uses the same retained replicates as every interval in the table. Rounded measurements and the median statistic create ties; a histogram bin is not a distinct population outcome.)
\alttext(A histogram of four thousand resampled iris sepal-length medians, centred around the observed median of 5.8 centimetres.)

> Calibration at increasing resample counts

The mean provides a reference without needing a coverage simulation. Each
row starts the same seeded engine and therefore uses a nested prefix of its
replicates on this toolchain. The solid reference is the exact conditional
bootstrap SE derived above. The second reference is s/sqrt(n), the usual
estimated mean SE. Monte Carlo deviations need not shrink monotonically with
B; their long-run limit is the conditional bootstrap reference.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bootstrap_mean_calibration.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();std::vector<double> x;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<iris.nrows();++i) x.push_back(iris.double_at("Sepal.Length",i));
  const double n=x.size(),s=dm::sd(x),conditional=s*std::sqrt(n-1)/n,textbook=s/std::sqrt(n);
  std::vector<double> log_b,observed,exact,analytic;
  std::print("| B | estimated bias | bootstrap SE | exact conditional SE | textbook SE |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t b:{200,1000,4000,16000}) {
    const auto result=dm::stats::Bootstrap({b,0.95,20240915}).run(x,[](const auto& values){return dm::mean(values);});
    log_b.push_back(std::log10(double(b)));observed.push_back(result.standard_error);
    exact.push_back(conditional);analytic.push_back(textbook);
    std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n",
        b,result.bias,result.standard_error,conditional,textbook);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(log_b,observed,"finite-B SE",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(log_b,observed,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .lines(log_b,exact,"conditional SE",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2)
      .lines(log_b,analytic,"textbook SE",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Mean calibration against the conditional bootstrap law")
      .x_label("log10(B); B = 200, 1000, 4000, 16000").y_label("standard error, cm")
      .y_limits(0.062,0.074).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bootstrap_mean_calibration.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| B | estimated bias | bootstrap SE | exact conditional SE | textbook SE |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 200 | 0.00602000 | 0.06552946 | 0.06738557 | 0.06761132 |
| 1000 | 0.00316467 | 0.06543574 | 0.06738557 | 0.06761132 |
| 4000 | -0.00004100 | 0.06701348 | 0.06738557 | 0.06761132 |
| 16000 | -0.00015863 | 0.06697893 | 0.06738557 | 0.06761132 |
\image(figures/bootstrap_mean_calibration.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Replicate dispersion is a conditional bootstrap quantity. The table reports both reference values to avoid confusing finite-n plug-in error with simulation error.)
\alttext(The estimated bootstrap standard error at four increasing resample counts is plotted against horizontal conditional-bootstrap and textbook mean standard-error references.)

The default overload uses a local MT19937-64 and the standard library's uniform
integer distribution. A fixed seed reproduces a run with the same implementation;
different C++ standard libraries may map raw engine words to indices differently.
A caller-owned `RandomGenerator` overload is also available. Neither overload
automatically handles dependence, clusters, informative sampling or a statistic
whose bootstrap distribution is inconsistent. Increasing B only improves the
Monte Carlo approximation to the selected conditional law.

> References

- B. Efron (1979), Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife,
  Annals of Statistics 7(1), 1–26. [Primary paper](https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552).
- B. Efron (1987), Better Bootstrap Confidence Intervals,
  Journal of the American Statistical Association 82(397), 171–185.
  [Primary paper](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1987.10478410).
- R stats, [median](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/median.html),
  for the familiar empty, missing and even-length median conventions.
